I read `PLATFORM.md`, `db.js`, `api/metrics.js`, `public/dashboard.js`, and `jobs/rollup.md`. The design is basically decided by one fact in `jobs/rollup.md`: the rollup job is the only writer and it runs once a minute. Between rewrites every row is byte-identical, and nothing in the system can know a number changed before that job runs. So the data has a 60-second heartbeat, and no transport can deliver news faster than the news exists.

## Why not push

Worth ruling out explicitly, since "make it live" usually means WebSockets:

- **WebSocket** is closed off — the edge returns 501 on the handshake, so it never reaches your code.
- **SSE** isn't an upgrade, so it wouldn't 501 — but the 10-second invocation cap kills the stream every 10 seconds and `EventSource` reconnects, giving you ~6 requests/minute per viewer to carry data that changes once. Strictly worse than polling once.
- **Postgres `LISTEN`/`NOTIFY`** can't work either: instances freeze between invocations, so the subscription stops the moment the response is sent.

Even if one of these worked, it would fire once a minute. Polling isn't the fallback here, it's the right answer.

## The build

Replace the load-once + button wiring in `dashboard.js` with a self-scheduling poll that locks onto the job's clock instead of running on a blind `setInterval`.

The scheduling should key off `computed_at`, not the browser clock — a user with a skewed clock would otherwise compute a garbage delay. Track it in local elapsed time:

```js
let lastComputedAt = null, lastSeenLocal = null, misses = 0;

async function tick() {
  const { rows, computed_at } = await (await fetch('/api/metrics')).json();
  if (computed_at !== lastComputedAt) {
    lastComputedAt = computed_at; lastSeenLocal = performance.now(); misses = 0;
    render(rows);                       // only on change - see below
  } else {
    misses++;                           // job late, or we arrived early
  }
  setTimeout(tick, nextDelay());
}
```

`nextDelay()` aims a couple seconds *before* the expected write (`lastSeenLocal + 60s - lead`), and on an unchanged response backs off short — 2s, 3s, 5s, capped around 10s, plus a few hundred ms of random jitter. That early-arrival-then-hunt loop self-corrects its phase: it converges on the real minute boundary without you hardcoding one, and it costs 1–3 cheap requests per minute. Chaining `setTimeout` rather than `setInterval` also means a slow or failed request can't stack up.

Two more client details:

- **Pause on `document.hidden`, refetch on `visibilitychange` → visible.** Background tabs get timer-throttled to roughly once a minute anyway, which would silently wreck the alignment; handling it explicitly makes the behavior intentional and stops idle tabs billing you invocations.
- **Only re-render when `computed_at` changes.** I can't see `render()`, so worth checking whether it rebuilds the DOM wholesale — a full replace under someone's cursor resets scroll and drops any text selection, which is a genuinely annoying regression when updates arrive unbidden.

## Server side

Small changes, mostly optional. The `no-store` in `api/metrics.js` means every viewer's poll is an invocation; because the rollup is global and identical for everyone, a short `s-maxage` (2–5s) would collapse origin load to a couple invocations per minute no matter how many dashboards are open, at the cost of a few seconds of staleness that the hunt loop absorbs fine. An `ETag` on `computed_at` with 304s is the alternative if you'd rather not cache bodies at the edge.

One real bug to fix first, since the whole scheme now keys on it: `rows[0] && rows[0].computed_at` yields `undefined` on an empty rollup, which the client turns into `Invalid Date`. Use `max(computed_at)` from the query and give the client a defined fallback.

## The thing this introduces

With a manual button, people know how old the data is because they just clicked. Once it updates on its own, a silently failing poll looks exactly like a quiet queue — which on an ops dashboard is the dangerous direction to fail in. So keep the last good render on error rather than blanking, and make the `stamp` element age-aware: normal when fresh, visibly degraded past ~2 minutes. I'd also keep the Refresh button as a force-now, both as an escape hatch and because people won't trust the auto-update for the first week.

Read the four files. The platform constraints and the rollup schedule between them settle the design, so let me lead with that.

## The constraint that decides it

`jobs/rollup.md` says the data changes **once a minute, on the minute**, from a single writer, and `db.js` confirms every row is byte-identical between rewrites. So the client doesn't need to be *told* when new data exists — it can compute when. That makes this a scheduling problem, not a transport problem, and push infrastructure would buy you nothing: any push system would be pushing a number that is already up to 60s old.

That also rules out what people normally reach for:

- **WebSockets** — `PLATFORM.md` says handshakes get 501 at the edge.
- **SSE** — survives the upgrade rule (it's a plain `text/event-stream` response, no `Upgrade` header), then dies on the 10s invocation cap. EventSource reconnects, so you've built a 10s poller with connection churn. And since instances are frozen and share no memory, the handler would query the DB on each connect anyway.
- **Long polling / `LISTEN`** — same 10s wall, and nothing to wake on.

## Aligned polling, anchored to server time

Poll once per minute, phased to land just after the rollup writes, with the schedule derived from `computed_at` rather than the browser clock — ops laptops have skew, and anchoring to `Date.now()` in the client would quietly drift the whole thing off-phase.

Have `api/metrics.js` do the subtraction server-side and add a conditional-request path. The `Cache-Control: no-store` there today is worth changing: it forbids the browser from storing the response at all, so it can never revalidate. `no-cache` lets it store-and-revalidate, which is what you want.

```js
const rows = await db.currentRollup();
const computed_at = rows[0] && rows[0].computed_at;
const tag = computed_at ? `"${new Date(computed_at).getTime()}"` : '"empty"';

res.setHeader('ETag', tag);
res.setHeader('Cache-Control', 'no-cache');
if (req.headers['if-none-match'] === tag) return res.status(304).end();

const age_ms = computed_at ? Date.now() - new Date(computed_at).getTime() : null;
res.json({ rows, computed_at, age_ms });
```

Since the payload is identical between rewrites, that ETag makes every off-phase or retry poll a 304 with no body instead of 4KB.

Client side:

```js
const PERIOD = 60_000, GUARD = 2_000;
const JITTER = Math.random() * 3_000;   // fixed per session: spreads the herd
let last = null, timer = null, misses = 0;

async function tick() {
  try {
    const { rows, computed_at, age_ms } = await (await fetch('/api/metrics')).json();
    if (computed_at && computed_at !== last) { last = computed_at; misses = 0; render(rows); }
    else misses++;                       // job ran late — or is dead
    paintFreshness(age_ms);
    schedule(misses
      ? Math.min(2_000 * 2 ** (misses - 1), 30_000) + JITTER
      : Math.max(PERIOD - (age_ms ?? PERIOD), 1_000) + GUARD + JITTER);
  } catch { schedule(Math.min(2_000 * 2 ** misses++, 30_000) + JITTER); }
}

function schedule(ms) { clearTimeout(timer); if (!document.hidden) timer = setTimeout(tick, ms); }
document.addEventListener('visibilitychange', () => { clearTimeout(timer); if (!document.hidden) tick(); });
```

A few things that sketch is deliberately doing:

- **Jitter.** Every dashboard aligning to the same boundary means a synchronized spike and a pile of cold starts each minute. A few seconds of spread costs nothing.
- **Visibility gating.** Ops dashboards live in forgotten background tabs. Chrome throttles hidden-tab timers to roughly 1/min, which is near your period but not reliable — handle it explicitly, and refetch on return since the data is certainly stale. A genuine wall display never hides, so it keeps polling.
- **Backoff on a repeated `computed_at`,** not just on network errors.

## The part that matters more than the polling

Today a failed fetch leaves the old numbers on screen with a confident-looking timestamp, forever. Auto-refresh makes that failure mode much worse, because nobody is clicking anything anymore to discover it's broken.

So drive a staleness state off `age_ms` — the data's own age, not "did my last fetch succeed." Past ~90s, visibly mark the dashboard stale. That distinction is the valuable one: it catches the rollup job being dead, which is currently invisible and is the failure that actually bites. It's also most of the perceived fix. The refresh button isn't annoying because it's a button; it's annoying because you can't tell whether the number in front of you is current.

Two smaller notes:

- **Empty-table hazard.** `rows[0] && rows[0].computed_at` yields `undefined` when `rollup` is empty, and a `NaN` delay makes `setTimeout` fire immediately — a hot loop against your API. The `?? PERIOD` and the `computed_at &&` guards above are load-bearing.
- **If concurrent dashboards ever cost you,** set `s-maxage` dynamically to the time remaining until the next boundary (`60 - age_seconds`, floored at 1) rather than a flat 60 — a flat value would expire mid-cycle and serve stale rows. That collapses all clients into ~1 origin query per minute. Optional at 30ms/4KB.

Worth keeping the Refresh button wired, incidentally — it's free and it's the escape hatch for anyone who doesn't trust the display. It just stops being the mechanism.
